  <section class="wrap fiche-body">
    <div>
      <h2>The vibe</h2>
      <p>Les Esclamandes beach, on the way out of Saint-Aygulf towards Fréjus, is split into four zones: family, naturist, kitesurf, and dogs. The dog zone, called Argens beach, occupies the northern end, near the Villepey ponds. Some consider it one of the most dog-friendly beaches in the region, with no time restriction year-round.</p>

      <h2>What to know before you go</h2>
      <p>Update: the contradiction we expected to find in our sources has been resolved. Both of Fréjus's official pages — the tourist office's ("beaches for dogs") and the municipal ville-frejus.fr site's — describe this dog area, near the Argens lifeguard post, without ever mentioning a time restriction: access is presented as possible year-round, dog on a leash. A third-party (non-official) source claimed a "before 9am and after 7pm" window that we couldn't find in any official text — worth flagging if you ever see different signage on site. Either way, remember to bring a sunshade, water and poop bags — little equipment is available on site.</p>
      <div class="callout">Coco's tip: the Villepey ponds right behind are a pleasant natural site to explore alongside a swim.</div>

      <h2>Nearby</h2>
      <p><a href="/en/fiche-plage-pacha-frejus">Plage du Pacha</a>, Fréjus's other official dog beach, is at the far end of the Base Nature — but not reachable on foot from here, as the Argens river mouth separates the two banks.</p>
    </div>

